        "year": 2023,
        "set": "6.2",
        "title": "Good habits every student should develop",
        "sourceText": "6.2 Good habits every student should develop A survey of educators highlighted ten habits that successful students share: 1. Get organised. Plan what you’re going to do and when you’re going to do it. 2. Don’t multitask. Studies have shown that multitasking is physically impossible. 3. Divide it up. Divide your work into manageable chunks and reward yourself when you finish each chunk. This will make studying (more) fun. 4. Set a schedule. Do you work better right after school or after you’ve eaten dinner? Are you more productive in 90-minute blocks or half-hour spurts? Find a schedule that works for you and stick to it. 5. Take notes. Taking notes will not only keep you more engaged during class, but also help you narrow down what you need to study. It’s much easier to reread your notes than to reread your entire textbook! 6. Review. Review your material several days ahead of time, in small chunks. Use flashcards or take practice tests. But don’t cram. 7. Manage your study space. Find a place away from the television and other distractions. Whether it’s the library or your bedroom, set aside a space that you’ll want to spend time in. 8. Find a study group. Get together with a group of people who are learning the same things. Teaching others helps you learn at the same time. 9. Ask questions. Don’t be afraid to ask questions or for help from a teacher or your friends. This will truly help you understand the material. 10. Sleep. Getting a good night’s rest will sharpen your focus and improve your working memory. Your group is preparing a sharing session for S.4 students to help them prepare for senior forms. You are meeting to discuss the topic. You may want to talk about:",
