      "sourceText": "Teaching money smarts to kids via cartoons Most children in Hong Kong get pocket money but, according to parents in a survey, it seems only a few believe their children have very good money management skills. Yet nearly all parents think it is important for kids to learn responsible money management skills, and would like tools to help them teach these skills. A new cartoon show — Cha-Ching Money Smart Kids — features a band called Cha-Ching whose aim is to teach children the basic concepts about money in a fun and engaging way. The programme stresses four basic money management concepts — earn, save, spend, and donate — so children learn the many ways they can use their pocket money. Most importantly, they are taught the differences between wants and needs. “Changing behaviour takes practice and Cha-Ching gives kids the chance to practise the four money management concepts. Ultimately, this practice will help build essential and responsible money management habits,” says the show’s creator. Your school is considering whether to show the Cha-Ching Money Smart Kids cartoon to teach good money management skills. Your group is discussing whether this is a good idea. You may want to talk about:",
